---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Shared payloads"
  description="A space-saving trick: multiple dialog entries pointing at the exact same payload. 53 percent of message files do this, and it's the reason naïve translations blow up to 5× their original size."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Message System', href: `${base}/field-guide/message-system/` },
    { label: 'Shared payloads' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    A message dialog file is a RESO container with 100 slots. Each
    slot has a length and an offset pointing at the actual dialog
    payload. Most of the time, every populated slot has its own
    unique payload.
  </p>

  <p>
    But not always. <strong>53 percent of message files (703 of 1,328)
    have at least one group of slots that share a single payload.</strong>
    The slot offsets all point at the same bytes. The engine reads
    the same dialog whether you index slot 0 or slot 9.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the game does this</h2>
  <p>
    Imagine ten NPCs in a town who all say "Welcome!" when you walk
    into their shop. The dialog is identical. Instead of writing ten
    copies of "Welcome!" into the file, the game writes it once and
    has ten table-of-contents entries all point at that one copy.
    Same engine behavior, less disk space.
  </p>

  <p>
    A real example, from <code>msg51016.ofs</code>: slots 0 through 9
    all share a single 158-byte payload. Slots 10 through 19 share a
    22-byte payload. Slots 20 through 29 share a 468-byte payload.
    The file has 100 TOC slots but only 27 distinct payloads behind
    them.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens when you translate naïvely</h2>
  <p>
    If your translation pipeline reads the file as 100 independent
    entries, translates each one separately, and writes each one back
    out as its own payload, you've just turned 27 unique payloads
    into up to 100 distinct payloads. The file balloons.
  </p>

  <p>
    For <code>msg51016.ofs</code>, the original Japanese file is
    3,472 bytes. A naïve translated version is 19,728 bytes — almost
    six times bigger. Multiply this across all the affected files and
    you've added megabytes of dialog data for no semantic reason.
  </p>

  <p>
    There's also a subtle quality problem. If your translator
    paraphrases each "Welcome!" differently, the same NPC might say
    "Welcome!" the first time you visit, "Hi there!" the second
    time, and "Hey!" the third. The original Japanese had them all
    saying the same thing on purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>The fix</h2>
  <p>
    There are two things to do, and together they recover most of
    the lost space:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Deduplicate byte-identical payloads.</strong> If two
      translated entries produce the same bytes by accident, write
      the bytes once and have both TOC slots point at it. This
      doesn't change any dialog at all — it's purely a structural
      win. About 7-8 percent recovery.</li>
    <li><strong>Canonicalize within original-shared groups.</strong>
      For each group of slots that shared a payload in the original
      file, pick one translation and use it for every slot in the
      group. This restores the original sharing pattern, matches the
      game's intent, and recovers about another 35 percent on top of
      the byte-dedup.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Both together recover roughly 1.5 MB of the 3.5 MB of size growth
    that an unaware translation produces. The translated patch is
    still bigger than the original (English text takes more bytes
    than Japanese text in UTF-16), but the multiplier is back down to
    about 1.3×, not 5×.
  </p>

  <h2>How to find the groups</h2>
  <p>
    Walking the original file's TOC. Group the entries by
    <code>(length, data_offset)</code> — every group with more than
    one entry is a shared-payload group. From there it's a matter of
    picking a winner translation and applying it to every slot in
    the group.
  </p>

  <p>
    We applied this canonicalization across all 1,328 files. It
    affected 2,890 groups and changed 6,726 dialog rows in the
    database, with zero rebuild-time structural mismatches.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
